00:37:20,080 --> 00:37:26,640
I know several personally from just, you know, being involved with basketball so long, you know, my neighbor's a college coach.

500
00:37:28,480 --> 00:37:32,800
I looked through a lot of my data, I looked through a lot of the feedback to kind of come to that decision to do that.

501
00:37:33,280 --> 00:37:36,480
Whereas before, I just kind of made what sounded interesting and fun to me.

502
00:37:36,800 --> 00:37:47,280
And so I kind of learned that lesson over the course of so many years, and I don't know why it took so long to learn the lesson of build what people actually need.

503
00:37:48,240 --> 00:37:58,320
And that sounds so incredibly obvious, but like when you sit at your computer and you've been at work all day or you've been a dad all day and you have this little nugget of time, going through feedback isn't really fun.

504
00:37:58,640 --> 00:38:06,080
Like you just want to build something that gets that creativity, that expression out or that new API that you want to use, you know, well, and your mind doesn't go there.

505
00:38:06,800 --> 00:38:13,680
I think there's a lot of there's a lot of advice online that is people saying, build what you love.

506
00:38:13,920 --> 00:38:16,240
The best thing, the best apps are the ones made by people.

507
00:38:16,800 --> 00:38:19,200
Solve your own problem that has the passion in it.

508
00:38:19,680 --> 00:38:20,480
Yeah, solve your own problem.

509
00:38:20,640 --> 00:38:22,000
And like, I don't think that's wrong.

510
00:38:22,400 --> 00:38:24,640
There's a lot of great examples of apps because I do.

511
00:38:25,200 --> 00:38:33,200
think you're generally, especially if you don't have a strong sort of product sense mentality to be clear.

512
00:38:34,080 --> 00:38:37,600
Yeah, then building something for yourself is way easier.

513
00:38:37,840 --> 00:38:39,920
You are a product expert in that field.

514
00:38:40,240 --> 00:38:41,360
You know yourself really well.

515
00:38:41,600 --> 00:38:42,640
You don't have to figure that out.

516
00:38:42,800 --> 00:38:52,880
So in a lot of ways it's a lot easier, but it's not strategic in terms of targeting a, an audience of any sort of size or a potential customer base of any sort of size.

517
00:38:53,760 --> 00:39:00,480
And so, yeah, some of the best apps I I have seen in terms of performance are people.

518
00:39:00,480 --> 00:39:06,560
Where people, whenever I talk to them, they kind of sheepishly are like, Yeah, I don't actually even really use it myself.

519
00:39:06,800 --> 00:39:06,880
Yeah.

520
00:39:07,200 --> 00:39:12,800
Like they sort of accidentally ended up in it or they made it for themselves when they were in college, but they're way out of that.

521
00:39:12,960 --> 00:39:14,480
They're not in that stage of life anymore.

522
00:39:15,200 --> 00:39:18,160
But they're still building excellent software for that group of people.

523
00:39:20,160 --> 00:39:22,240
And I think that's just as valid.

524
00:39:22,400 --> 00:39:28,320
Sometimes it's even beneficial to be a little bit outside of it because you're not building something particular to you.

525
00:39:28,480 --> 00:39:32,640
You're a lot more open to actually targeting it towards the market.

526
00:39:33,040 --> 00:39:33,040
Yeah.

527
00:39:33,520 --> 00:39:35,200
No, that's completely true.

528
00:39:36,320 --> 00:39:41,120
Just like, you know, at the end of the day, people are going to pay for something that really solves their problem.

529
00:39:41,520 --> 00:39:43,280
And I know everyone's heard that a million times.

530
00:39:43,520 --> 00:39:46,240
It's on every product podcast, it's on every product blog post.

531
00:39:46,800 --> 00:39:50,720
But like, what, I don't know why it just never went through my head, you know?

532
00:39:51,520 --> 00:40:04,480
And again, I think we're in a very specific niche of people that love making software on their own, to where like, your creative energy is kind of trashed by the time you have time to actually work on this stuff, you know?

533
00:40:04,880 --> 00:40:08,000
So it got to the point where I was like, I'm just going to be ruthless about this.

534
00:40:08,400 --> 00:40:12,480
Like, if I can't make this make money, I'm not going to work on it.

535
00:40:14,240 --> 00:40:23,760
And now it's when you look at it as more of a product and less in an indie app, that's been the shift that I personally needed to kind of grow it and to make it better.

536
00:40:25,200 --> 00:40:26,800
And you know, I bootstrap all of it.

537
00:40:26,960 --> 00:40:37,040
And it's an interesting time to be talking about it actually because I think in the group chat the other day, I was like, hey, you know, that was my best day on Elite Hoops, like $645 in one day.

538
00:40:38,560 --> 00:40:39,200
Something like that.

539
00:40:40,000 --> 00:40:44,320
But since then, it's it's kept that pace like within a few hundred dollars every day.

540
00:40:45,680 --> 00:40:48,960
And it's been so exciting to see like the momentum actually really start to pay off.

541
00:40:50,240 --> 00:41:00,720
And one shift that I made is like, before if I had any kind of side project income, you know, it was just that, like, you know, I'll use it for vacation, you know, just some nice to have stuff.

542
00:41:00,880 --> 00:41:04,800
You know, it's not that much money, but it's, it's nice to have that little extra income.

543
00:41:05,440 --> 00:41:10,480
Um, that you don't just dump it into a account for fear of the tax man for years like I did.

544
00:41:10,880 --> 00:41:13,440
Charlie has six trillion dollars saved up for anyone listening.

545
00:41:13,520 --> 00:41:19,600
And, uh, I have been like, Yeah, you're too scared to spend any of it, but six trillion, it's all under a mattress in my house.

546
00:41:20,480 --> 00:41:26,000
But what I've done with a lot of the money that I've been making with Elite Hoops is I'm pouring it right back into the product.

547
00:41:26,480 --> 00:41:43,360
So for example, I started with $30 in Apple search ads when it first launched and my little logic was like, if I just get one conversion like baby steps, if I can just get one person to convert for $45, then I mean, yeah, you got to take taxes, all that stuff away, but like at least I come out on top.

548
00:41:43,600 --> 00:41:59,200
So like I started there and I've just slowly scaled up as the years have gone on to where now I spend $160 in advertising every single day just on autopilot and then I've seen the revenue go up to like $500 or $600.

549
00:42:00,320 --> 00:42:04,400
At the turn of the year, I'll look at it again and be like, Okay, so should I up this to 200 to 300?

550
00:42:04,560 --> 00:42:08,960
And then if the revenue scales with it, then I'll just, I'll just keep going until it doesn't work.

551
00:42:10,000 --> 00:42:24,240
But that only works if your lifetime value per customer that you're acquiring through those is lower than or is higher than whatever the cost of that ad was, right?

552
00:42:25,120 --> 00:42:25,680
Yeah, I guess.

553
00:42:25,840 --> 00:42:27,200
I don't really look at charts very much.

554
00:42:28,080 --> 00:42:31,760
I look at this is where my product knowledge goes out the window.

555
00:42:31,920 --> 00:42:35,520
I look at MRR and I like, I love looking at what I make every day.

556
00:42:36,240 --> 00:42:53,120
Um, so that's the way I've looked at it, you know, as long as I get the conversion today, you know, then, then I'll Yeah, but the thing, the thing is, what you don't know is if, if you turn those off, would your revenue go down more or less than the amount you're spending for sure.

557
00:42:53,280 --> 00:42:54,480
And that's where you really have to experiment.

558
00:42:54,800 --> 00:43:00,720
So I did that and I just kept a simple Excel spreadsheet and I, I think I divided it up in like four things.

559
00:43:00,880 --> 00:43:02,160
This was like four months ago.

560
00:43:02,960 --> 00:43:08,560
Uh, and the slots were just like organic, you know, no Apple search ads, no meta ads, nothing.

561
00:43:09,280 --> 00:43:13,440
Just Apple search ads, just meta ads, just an Instagram boostosted post.

